---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import B from "../data/bynames.json";

const und = B.rows.filter((r) => r.underlined === "u");
const conn = [...new Set(B.rows.map((r) => r.conn).filter((c) => c !== "—"))];
---
<Base title="The Second Name"
  eyebrow={`${B.rows.length} phrases · ${B.nPairs} pairs · ${B.nSurnames} surnames · ${B.years[0]}–${B.years[1]}`}
  dek="A household by-name is not a nickname and not a spelling. It is a second name that identifies which family of that surname you mean — and in these parishes the priest wrote both, joined them with a word, and underlined the join.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This page exists because a question got a bigger answer than it asked for.</p>
    <p><a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 5</a> asked whether <strong>Antić</strong> and
    <strong>Papić</strong> were one family or two, and whether <em>Papa</em> was a surname or
    something else. The answer is that <em>Papa</em> is a <strong>nadimak</strong> — a house
    by-name — and the surname it sits beside is Antić.</p>
    <p>But the evidence turned out not to be about this family at all. Reading the registers at
    full resolution in September 2026 found the same construction <strong>{B.rows.length} times,
    across {B.nSurnames} surnames and {B.places.length} parishes</strong>, over
    {B.years[1] - B.years[0]} years and at least three different priests' hands.
    <strong>Papa is one instance of a system.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The tell</div>
    <h2>The scribes underlined them</h2>
    <p class="lede">{B.nUnderlined} of the {B.rows.length} phrases are <strong>underlined in the
    register itself</strong>. Not the archive's emphasis — the priest's.</p>
    <p>That is what makes this a finding rather than a guess. A scribe who underlines
    <em>Antich Papa</em>, <em>Lonzarich Franich</em> and <em>Jellichich dictis Jurešina</em> on his
    own pages is not writing down two surnames by accident. <strong>He is marking a convention he
    expects a later reader to need</strong> — and two hundred years later, that reader is this
    archive.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">Where a connecting word is used it is
    {conn.map((c, i) => (<><em class="mono">{c}</em>{i < conn.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>))} — Latin
    for <em>otherwise</em>, <em>called</em>, and in one case <em>adopted</em>. Often there is no
    word at all and the two names simply run together.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Every phrase read</h2>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th class="mono">year</th><th>As written</th><th>Surname</th><th>By-name</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{B.rows.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">{r.year}</td>
          <td><span class="mono">{r.written}</span>
            {r.underlined === "u" && <span class="tag" style="margin-left:.5rem">underlined</span>}
            <div class="dim" style="margin-top:.25rem">{r.source}</div></td>
          <td><strong>{r.surname}</strong></td>
          <td><strong>{r.byname}</strong></td>
          <td class="dek">{r.place}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What it is for</h2>
    <p class="lede">A village where forty households share nine surnames cannot be run on surnames.
    The by-name is the thing that tells <strong>which Antić</strong> you mean.</p>
    <p>It is the same job the <a href={u("/gazetteer/")}>house number</a> does at Smokvica, and the
    same job the patronymic does at Otočac — <em class="mono">Ivo Blažević <strong>sin
    Tomin</strong></em> against <em class="mono">Ivo Blažević <strong>sin Matin</strong></em>, two
    men of one name told apart by their fathers. Three devices, one problem.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">And at <a href={u("/harvest/")}>Grižane in 1738</a> the census
    does it a fourth way: <em class="mono">Juraj car <strong>Vulin</strong></em>, <em class="mono">Matij
    car <strong>Jurišin</strong></em>, <em class="mono">Lovro car <strong>Mikulin</strong></em> —
    <em>of Vule, of Juraj, of Mikula</em> — and one household identified purely by marriage,
    <em class="mono">Mikula … <strong>Blaškovicha zet</strong></em>, Blašković's son-in-law.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why it matters beyond one question</div>
    <h2>A by-name can eat a surname</h2>
    <p class="lede">The Antići are the proof. Watch the same family across five documents:</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th class="mono">1806</th><td><strong>Antich Papa</strong> — surname first, by-name second, underlined</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="mono">1835</th><td><strong>Josephum Antich Papa</strong> — still both, underlined</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="mono">1835</th><td><strong>Euphemia Antich <em>aliter</em> Papa</strong> — his sister, the join spelled out</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="mono">1838</th><td><strong>Josephi Antich Papa</strong> — still both, at his son's font</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="mono">1885</th><td><strong>Juraj Papa</strong> — <strong>the surname is gone</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">By 1885 the priest at Senj writes only the by-name, and every
    descendant after him is written <strong>Papić</strong>. <strong>The house name became the
    family name in three generations</strong>, and the registers caught it happening.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>The warning in it.</strong> If a by-name can replace a surname, then
    two records of one family can disagree about its name without either being wrong — and two
    families can look like one because they share a by-name. Any match made on a surname in these
    parishes is weaker than it looks. That is the rule
    <a href={u("/duplicates/")}>the duplicate finder</a> is built around, and this is where it
    comes from.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Checked 14 September 2026</div>
    <h2>Does this matter to the other six archives?</h2>
    <p class="lede">Six sibling archives share a family tree with this one. <strong>On the names,
    no.</strong> Of the ten surnames carrying a by-name here, exactly one — <strong>Papić</strong> —
    appears in any sibling archive's surname list, and that archive is this one.</p>
    <p><strong>On the method, quite possibly yes.</strong> The Defranceschi archive covers
    <em>Istria · Kvarner · Carnia</em> — and Kvarner is the same coast: Krk, Vinodol, the parishes
    whose priests wrote <em>aliter</em> and underlined it. A convention this consistent across ten
    surnames and three parishes is unlikely to stop at the edge of one family's records.</p>
    <p class="dek">Recorded as a negative result rather than left as an open loop. Nothing has been
    changed in another archive on the strength of it.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
